Delivery provider scope and reload behavior
==========================================

Loading providers for delivery is now distinct from administrative listing.
NotificationService.load_providers_from_db(organization_id) loads only enabled
providers owned by that exact organization. An organization_id of None selects
only platform-owned records; it no longer means every tenant's provider.
Administrative list_providers(None) retains its existing all-tenant behavior
behind the caller's authorization checks.

A reload clears the old runtime map before resolving the database records and
only installs the replacement map after the complete load succeeds. A failed
lookup or provider construction cannot silently fall back to an old tenant's
credentials. Disabled or removed providers disappear on the next reload.
Defaults are preferred, then newer records, with a stable ID tie-breaker.

Compatibility: manually registered runtime providers are not retained across a
database reload. Configure such providers explicitly after a successful load,
or keep them on a separately scoped service. Platform-level sends now require a
platform-owned provider; they will not borrow a tenant's configuration.

This does not turn an already loaded service into a live revocation subscription.
Provider changes after the lookup are not atomically fenced with external sends.
Do not share one service/session across concurrent organization reloads. Broker
durability, dispatch transaction ownership and live provider acceptance remain
separate release work. Administrative listing is not permission to send with a
provider selected implicitly from another organization.

NOTIFICATION PROVIDER MANAGEMENT: OWNERSHIP AND CONCURRENCY
=========================================================

Default promotion demotes providers only in the same owning organization and
channel. A NULL organization is the platform-provider group, not all tenants.
An unscoped platform administrator may retain all-tenant management access;
updating a tenant provider still uses that provider's actual organization when
choosing which other defaults to demote. Listing and lookup permissions are
unchanged. Scoped API keys do not gain the unscoped administrator role.

Service create/update/delete calls take a PostgreSQL transaction advisory lock
for the actual owner, including the platform group. This covers empty groups
where locking an existing default row would provide no protection. Callers
must commit or roll back their transaction. Writes for different organizations
use different scope keys. After waiting, update/delete reload the stored row,
refuse a missing or moved provider and merge config edits with current committed
values. A delayed metadata edit therefore cannot restore a superseded secret.
Sensitive configuration remains encrypted and API summaries remain masked.

The serialization applies to these management service methods. It is not a
database unique constraint, does not make independent raw SQL or restore writers
participate, and does not continuously revalidate the caller's session, API key,
role or grants. Historical duplicate defaults are not globally rewritten;
explicit promotion demotes other providers in that owner/channel. Defaults in
other channels and organizations are preserved. Notification transport, pending
delivery, exactly-once effects and current delivery authority have separate
controls; management tests send no real notification.

The regression evidence uses real independent PostgreSQL transactions and an
isolated TCP API with ordinary JWT/API-key dependencies. Fixture identities and
tokens are provisioned directly. This is not password-login, production middleware,
external provider, hardware, customer recovery or enterprise release acceptance.


CONCURRENT PROVIDER VERIFICATION
===============================

A verification or test send may finish after a provider was edited, disabled,
deleted or moved to another owner. The service snapshots its original owner,
provider type/channel, enabled state, update timestamp and encrypted configuration
before transport I/O. It records verification/error metadata only when an atomic
conditional UPDATE still matches that binding. A brief owner advisory lock is
acquired after I/O; management operations do not wait on the network probe.

A stale verification returns an explicit retry message instead of certifying the
new configuration. A completed test send retains its actual transport outcome,
but does not mark a changed provider verified or overwrite its diagnostics. The
service cannot recall a send already dispatched to the former configuration.

These checks do not fence transport side effects against in-flight caller/grant
revocation, establish actual SMTP/provider interoperability, or guarantee durable
or exactly-once delivery. Snapshot restoration and an exact raw-SQL restoration
of the old binding are not a globally monotonic configuration-generation fence.
